    {
      "kind": "tool",
      "tool": "govinfo.house_report",
      "call": "Fetch GovInfo H. Rept. 119-698 content page and lines around summary/appropriations relationship",
      "result": "GovInfo H. Rept. 119-698 title is NATIONAL DEFENSE AUTHORIZATION ACT FOR FISCAL YEAR 2027; the report says it accompanies H.R. 8800. It states the President requested $1.15 trillion for national defense budget function 050, $1.14 trillion within committee jurisdiction, $1.1 trillion for Department of Defense programs, $41.4 billion for DOE national security programs, and $568 million for Maritime Administration defense-related activities. It also states the committee authorization is $251.1 billion above FY2026 NDAA levels and that the bill does not provide budget authority; subsequent appropriations acts provide budget authority."
    },
    {
      "kind": "tool",
      "tool": "thesis.specs_check",
      "call": "curl -sS -D - https://app.thesisinstitute.org/specs.json -o /tmp/thesis-specs.json; rg slug",
      "result": "Slug check fetch returned HTTP 404 with content-length 11289 from app.thesisinstitute.org/specs.json; local rg found slug_matches=0 for us-dod-prime-award-obligations-fy2027-fy27-ndaa-enacted. I therefore used the provided registered catalogSlug rather than inventing a new slug."
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Base rate / reference class: comparable complete registered-query prints are FY2017-FY2025 obligations values of 328.0B, 365.9B, 391.3B, 431.9B, 395.3B, 424.5B, 467.3B, 456.9B, and 501.5B. The reference class has mean level 418.1B, median level 424.5B, range 328.0B-501.5B, mean annual change +21.7B, median annual change +33.6B, and annual-change sigma 29.5B. Last-print persistence is the strongest simple benchmark I can defend for this registered series: 501.5B from FY2025."
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Model candidates under thesis_model_candidate_v1, summarized in trace: persistence candidate has point 501.5, p10 463.8, p50 501.5, p90 539.3, 80% interval [463.8, 539.3], 90% interval [453.2, 549.9], interval_method annual-change residual normal, calibration_n 8, train_cutoff FY2025, and walk_forward_mae 33.4. A median-change drift candidate would be 535.1, but the FY2027 authorization evidence is not direct budget authority for prime awards, so I select a shrinkage forecast halfway from persistence toward median historical growth."
    },
    {
      "kind": "math",
      "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence prior = 501.5B from FY2025; historical sample = FY2017-FY2025 complete registered-query values with annual changes 37.9, 25.4, 40.5, -36.6, 29.2, 42.8, -10.3, and 44.6B; adjustment components = +16.8B, equal to 50% of the +33.6B median annual change, because conditional FY2027 NDAA enactment and the House report's $1.1T DoD-program request support an upward path but the report also says authorization is not budget authority. Interval method = annual-change residual, sigma = 29.5B, nominal 80% half-width = 1.28*sigma = 1.28*29.5 = 37.7B; I widen by 1.5x to 56.6B because the forecast is two fiscal years ahead and depends on authorization-to-appropriation execution. Point = 501.5 + 16.8 = 518.3B; using unrounded point 518.325 and half-width 56.565 gives interval [461.8, 574.9]B."
    },
    {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "Sanity check: a +/-56.6B band around each previous-year print would have covered all 8 of the FY2018-FY2025 realized annual changes. Downside risk outside the interval would be an enacted NDAA followed by appropriations delay, award timing slippage, deobligation cleanup, or a registered snapshot that still misses late-FY DoD award reporting. Upside risk outside the interval would be an unusually large procurement/RDT&E award closeout, supplemental defense appropriations, or faster USAspending posting that would land above the interval."
    },
